    "summary": "High‑energy Nassau show with soaring Dark Star and monster Playin’ In The Band",
    "blurb": "The first set crackles with a classic China Cat Sunflower > I Know You Rider sandwich, tight rhythm work and bright vocals. The second set builds through The Promised Land into an expansive Dark Star that segues into Truckin' → Morning Dew, ending on a jubilant Sugar Magnolia. Soundboard recordings capture the band at peak intensity with crisp mix and minimal flaws.",
    "review": "March 16, 1973 at Nassau Coliseum finds the Dead in top form, delivering a two‑set marathon that still feels fresh. Jerry’s guitar tone is flawless throughout, from the lyrical phrasing on China Cat Sunflower to the searing, “serious licks” that drive the Dark Star and Playin’ In The Band jams. Phil’s bass is a “smoke‑show,” bomb‑like and melodic, anchoring each improvisational stretch while adding strong backing vocals. Bob Weir provides solid rhythm guitar and vocal highlights, especially on Looks Like Rain and the seamless China > Rider transition. Keith Godchaux’s piano adds depth and mojo to the second‑set flow, complementing the tight drumming of Bill Kreutzmann, whose intensity propels the extended Dark Star → Truckin' → Morning Dew sequence. The setlist moves effortlessly from upbeat rockers to expansive explorations, culminating in a joyous Sugar Magnolia encore that leaves the audience buzzing.",
